    "result_text": "The change is complete and the full build passes.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented per-country national-number length validation for E.164 telephones in `OwnerTelephone.toE164` (`src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/OwnerTelephone.java`).\n\n**What changed:** After normalizing to the digit string and applying the existing general 8\u201315 digit bound, the code now checks the national-number length against the country code:\n- `+61` \u2192 must have exactly 9 national digits\n- `+1` \u2192 must have exactly 10 national digits\n\nThe `\"61\"` prefix is checked before `\"1\"` so an Australian number isn't misread as a US one. A wrong length throws the existing checked `OwnerTelephoneInvalidException`, which `OwnerTelephoneInvalidExceptionHandler` already maps to a **400** with `{\"errors\":[\"telephone\"]}`. Numbers with other country codes keep the general 8\u201315 rule as before. I also updated the class Javadoc to document the new rule.\n\n**Tests:** The new `AcceptanceTest.errorRejectsWrongNationalLength` (posts `\"+61 123\"`, expecting 400) passes, and the full `./mvnw test` suite is green (BUILD SUCCESS). The seed telephones (`608...`) are inserted directly via SQL and never pass through `toE164`, and `uniqueTelephone()` produces a valid 9-national-digit `+61` number, so existing owner-creation flows remain valid.",
